.SH Schema in detail
This page adds the details to ../schema.md.
\f[CR]cbor::schema<T>\f[R] is one class for every compiler: it builds
with C++23 on g++ 15, g++ 16, clang 23 and AppleClang.
With C++26 reflection (g++ 16 with \f[CR]\-std=c++26 \-freflection\f[R])
it also checks each description against the members that reflection
reads.
The examples use these structs:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] tire {
    std::uint16_t diameter;
    float pressure;
};

\f[B]struct\f[R] car {
    std::string make;
    std::uint8_t seats;
    std::vector<tire> tires;
    std::vector<double> track;
    std::optional<std::string> note;
};

CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(tire, cbor::tag{1700}, diameter, pressure);
CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(car, cbor::tag{1701}, make, seats, tires, track, note);

car const sample{\(dqVolvo\(dq, 5, {{17, 2.5f}, {18, 2.25f}}, {1.0, 2.0, 3.0}, std::nullopt};
\f[B]auto\f[R] const owner = std::make_shared<std::string const>(*cbor::schema<car>::encode(sample));
std::string_view const bytes = *owner;                    \f[I]// the message, read in place\f[R]
.EE
.SS 1. The message
A message is one tag 113 (Basic\-Packed\-CBOR,
draft\-ietf\-cbor\-packed\-19) on an array of a table and a rump.
.IP \(bu 2
The table holds the keys of each struct as a tag 114 (the record
function).
For each struct that is the element of a list of fixed\-size structs, it
holds a description: the tag of the struct and the key and RFC 8746 type
of each member.
Then come a directory of the offsets of the parts of variable size, and
those parts: texts, byte strings and lists.
.IP \(bu 2
The rump is the root struct.
It starts with the tag of its struct.
Each field has a head of fixed width, so a number takes the same count
of bytes for every value.
A float field holds the bits of the value at the width of its type, so a
NaN keeps its bits.
.IP \(bu 2
A list of a fixed\-width number is a typed array of RFC 8746 in little
endian.
.IP \(bu 2
A list of fixed\-size structs, such as \f[CR]tires\f[R], is one byte
string of its elements under tag 32873: little endian, with no padding.
.PP
A decoder of Packed CBOR reads the message as a map with the names of
the members as keys.
\f[CR]cbor::item_size\f[R] of a message is the size of the message.
\&../../reference/schema/wire.md shows each byte.
.PP
\f[CR]cbor::schema<T>::description()\f[R] gives the 114 entries and the
descriptions.
They are the same in every message of \f[CR]T\f[R].
With \f[CR]cbor::description_place::apart\f[R], \f[CR]encode\f[R] writes
the message without them, and only a read with \f[CR]apart\f[R] reads
that message (section 3).
.SS 2. Structs and tags
The root and every struct inside it have a description with a tag.
\f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE(type, tag, members...)\f[R] writes it at global
scope.
The tag numbers differ.
\f[CR]cbor::tags_registered<T>()\f[R] is true when this holds.
A struct with no description, or two structs with one tag, do not
compile in \f[CR]schema<T>\f[R].
.PP
The macro writes a specialization of \f[CR]cbor::describe_members\f[R].
For \f[CR]tire\f[R], it writes:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]template\f[R] <>
\f[B]struct\f[R] cbor::describe_members<tire> {
    static \f[B]constexpr\f[R] std::optional<cbor::tag> tag = cbor::tag{1700};
    static \f[B]constexpr\f[R] std::tuple members{CBOR_MEMBER(tire, diameter), CBOR_MEMBER(tire, pressure)};
};
.EE
.PP
