.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] chosen {
    std::uint8_t number;
    std::uint8_t kept;
};

CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE(chosen, cbor::tag{1710}, cbor::member{\(dqid\(dq, &chosen::number}, CBOR_MEMBER(chosen, kept));

\f[B]auto\f[R] const message = cbor::schema<chosen>::encode(chosen{7, 8});
cbor::schema<chosen>::at_path<\(dq$.id\(dq>(*message);   \f[I]// 7\f[R]
.EE
.PP
A class with a base class names its direct base in its description:
\f[CR]using base = animal;\f[R] in \f[CR]describe_members<dog>\f[R], or
\f[CR]CBOR_DEFINE_DERIVED_TYPE_MEMBERS(dog, animal, cbor::tag{1001}, name, bark)\f[R].
The description lists the members of the base too.
With reflection, the named base is the one direct base of the class.
Without reflection, \f[CR]std::is_base_of_v\f[R] checks it, so an
indirect base is not found there, and Boost.PFR does not count the
members of such a class.
A class with a named base is not an aggregate that takes an allocator.
.PP
A \f[CR]std::variant\f[R] member takes as alternatives classes with
their own tag, numbers, \f[CR]bool\f[R], enums with a fixed underlying
type, texts and byte strings.
The class alternatives name one and the same direct base:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] home {
    std::variant<dog, cat> pet;
};

\f[B]struct\f[R] property {
    std::string name;
    std::variant<bool, std::int64_t, std::string> value;
};
.EE
.PP
The CBOR head of the item chooses the alternative, so two alternatives
whose items begin with one head do not compile: \f[CR]std::int32_t\f[R]
with \f[CR]std::uint32_t\f[R], \f[CR]float\f[R] with
\f[CR]std::bfloat16_t\f[R], an enum with its underlying type, two text
types.
A list, a map, an array or a \f[CR]std::optional\f[R] of a variant does
not compile, and neither does one as an alternative.
A variant with \f[CR]std::monostate\f[R], with a class that has no
description or no tag, with one type twice, or with classes of other
bases does not compile.
.PP
These do not compile: a key that is not a text, two members with one
key, a member listed twice, \f[CR]cbor::untagged\f[R], and a tag that a
standard type has.
With reflection, a member that the description leaves out and a class
with a base class that the description does not name do not compile
either.
Without reflection, Boost.PFR counts the members of an aggregate with no
named base where it is available, and a count that differs does not
compile.
.PP
A class with no default constructor is read through a constructor that
takes the described members in the order of the description.
