.PP
\f[CR]decode\f[R] takes the same forms as \f[CR]structs<T>::decode\f[R]:
a moved \f[CR]std::string\f[R], a
\f[CR]std::shared_ptr<std::string const>\f[R], a
\f[CR]std::shared_ptr<void const>\f[R] with a view of the bytes, and
each of the last two with a target (structs.md, section 2).
No form copies the bytes.
A target of \f[CR]std::pmr\f[R] containers sets the memory of the result
(basics.md, section 6).
.PP
\f[CR]encode\f[R] writes a list of fixed\-size structs with one copy
where the struct in memory has the bytes of the element: little endian,
no padding, and each member at its offset.
\f[CR]tire\f[R] has two bytes of padding, so \f[CR]encode\f[R] writes
each of its elements field by field.
A compile\-time probe finds the layout, on every compiler.
.PP
Each call takes \f[CR]cbor::description_place\f[R] as a template
argument: the first one, or the one after the path in
\f[CR]at_path\f[R].
The default is \f[CR]inside\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]constexpr\f[R] \f[B]auto\f[R] apart = cbor::description_place::apart;
\f[B]auto\f[R] const data = cbor::schema<car>::encode<apart>(sample);
cbor::schema<car>::decode<apart>(std::string(*data));                        \f[I]// the car\f[R]
data\->size() + cbor::schema<car>::description().size() == bytes.size();     \f[I]// true\f[R]
.EE
.SS 4. One value with no accessor
\f[CR]schema<T>::at_path<Path>(encoded, index...)\f[R] reads one value.
It makes every check of an accessor and keeps nothing.
It exists only where the value holds no view into the message: a number,
a \f[CR]bool\f[R], an enum, or an optional of one.
.IP
.EX
cbor::schema<car>::at_path<\(dq$.seats\(dq>(bytes);                      \f[I]// 5\f[R]
cbor::schema<car>::at_path<\(dq$.tires[1].diameter\(dq>(bytes);          \f[I]// 18\f[R]
cbor::schema<car>::at_path<\(dq$.tires[].pressure\(dq>(bytes, 0uz);      \f[I]// 2.5f\f[R]
cbor::schema<car>::at_path<\(dq$.track[2]\(dq>(bytes);                   \f[I]// 3.0\f[R]
cbor::schema<car>::at_path<\(dq$.tires[].diameter\(dq>(bytes, 5uz);      \f[I]// error::index_out_of_bounds\f[R]
.EE
.PP
A path is a string literal.
It names members with \f[CR].name\f[R] and elements with \f[CR][n]\f[R].
\f[CR][]\f[R] takes its index from the next argument, a
\f[CR]std::size_t\f[R], so the count of arguments equals the count of
\f[CR][]\f[R].
This path is not JSONPath: it has no wildcard, no filter and no
descendant segment.
.PP
On a byte string, a \f[CR]std::array\f[R] or a \f[CR]std::vector\f[R] of
\f[CR]std::uint8_t\f[R] or \f[CR]std::byte\f[R], \f[CR][n]\f[R] reads
one byte:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]struct\f[R] frame {
    std::array<std::uint8_t, 4> code;
    std::vector<std::byte> payload;
};

CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(frame, cbor::tag{1711}, code, payload);

\f[B]auto\f[R] const packet = cbor::schema<frame>::encode(frame{{1, 2, 3, 4}, {std::byte{9}, std::byte{8}}});
cbor::schema<frame>::at_path<\(dq$.code[2]\(dq>(*packet);      \f[I]// 3\f[R]
cbor::schema<frame>::at_path<\(dq$.payload[1]\(dq>(*packet);   \f[I]// std::byte{8}\f[R]
cbor::schema<frame>::at_path<\(dq$.payload[]\(dq>(*packet, 2uz);   \f[I]// error::index_out_of_bounds\f[R]
.EE
.PP
A path to a member that \f[CR]T\f[R] does not have, a wrong count of
arguments, a path to a text, a constant index past the end of a
\f[CR]std::array\f[R], or a path to a member that is not trivially
copyable, such as a \f[CR]std::vector\f[R] of structs, does not compile.
.SS 5. The accessor
\f[CR]schema<T>::path(...)\f[R] opens a message and gives the root
accessor, \f[CR]schema<T>::accessor<T>\f[R].
The forms are those of \f[CR]decode\f[R]:
