.PP
An accessor holds the owner.
Every value and every accessor that it gives holds the same owner, so an
accessor of a list outlives the temporary root that gave it.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]auto\f[R] const root = cbor::schema<car>::path(owner);
root\->at_path<\(dq$.make\(dq>();                  \f[I]// \(dqVolvo\(dq, a std::string_view\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const tires = root\->at_path<\(dq$.tires\(dq>();
tires\->size();                              \f[I]// 2\f[R]
\f[B]auto\f[R] const second = tires\->at_path<\(dq\(at[]\(dq>(1uz);
second\->at_path<\(dq\(at.diameter\(dq>();            \f[I]// 18\f[R]
second\->at_path<\(dq$.seats\(dq>();               \f[I]// 5\f[R]
root\->at_path<\(dq$.note\(dq>();                  \f[I]// an empty std::optional<std::string_view>\f[R]
.EE
.PP
A path that starts with \f[CR]$\f[R] starts at the root.
A path that starts with \f[CR]\(at\f[R] starts at the part that the
accessor names.
\f[CR]size()\f[R] gives the count of elements of a list or a map.
.PP
The accessor of a list of fixed\-size structs is a
\f[CR]std::ranges::random_access_range\f[R] of element accessors:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]auto\f[R] const t : *tires | std::views::drop(1))
    t.at_path<\(dq\(at.pressure\(dq>();              \f[I]// 2.25f\f[R]
.EE
.PP
A \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] stays valid while an accessor of the same
owner lives.
\f[CR]at_path\f[R] on a temporary accessor does not compile.
An empty owner throws \f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
An accessor keeps its view after a move, and \f[CR]at_path\f[R] on the
accessor that it was moved from throws \f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SS 6. Typed arrays
\f[CR]accessor<T>::view<Path>(index...)\f[R] gives a
\f[CR]typed_array_view<E>\f[R] of a list of a fixed\-width number E. The
view holds the owner and reads each element from the bytes of the
message.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]auto\f[R] const track = root\->view<\(dq$.track\(dq>();
track\->size();                                          \f[I]// 3\f[R]
std::accumulate(track\->begin(), track\->end(), 0.0);     \f[I]// 6.0\f[R]
(*track)[1];                                            \f[I]// 2.0\f[R]
track\->at(3);                                           \f[I]// error::index_out_of_bounds\f[R]
(*track)[3];                                            \f[I]// throws std::logic_error\f[R]
track\->last(2)\->front();                                \f[I]// 2.0\f[R]
track\->first(4);                                        \f[I]// error::index_out_of_bounds\f[R]
std::ranges::fold_left(*track, 0.0, std::plus<>{});     \f[I]// 6.0\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]typed_array_view<E>\f[R] is a
\f[CR]std::ranges::random_access_range\f[R] and a
\f[CR]std::ranges::sized_range\f[R].
It has \f[CR]size\f[R], \f[CR]empty\f[R], \f[CR]begin\f[R],
\f[CR]end\f[R], \f[CR]rbegin\f[R], \f[CR]rend\f[R], \f[CR]front\f[R],
\f[CR]back\f[R], \f[CR]first\f[R], \f[CR]last\f[R], \f[CR]at\f[R] and
\f[CR]operator[]\f[R].
Each element is a value of E, not a reference.
\f[CR]at\f[R], \f[CR]first\f[R] and \f[CR]last\f[R] give an error as a
value.
\f[CR]operator[]\f[R], \f[CR]front\f[R] and \f[CR]back\f[R] throw
\f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R] outside the view.
\f[CR]begin\f[R] and \f[CR]end\f[R] do not compile on a temporary view.
.SS 7. What is checked once and what at each read
\f[CR]path\f[R] checks once:
.IP \(bu 2
that the message is at least as long as the table and the root;
.IP \(bu 2
that the start of the table holds the keys of \f[CR]T\f[R] and of each
struct inside it, and the descriptions, so a message of another layout
gives \f[CR]incorrect_type\f[R];
.IP \(bu 2
that the directory lies inside the message;
.IP \(bu 2
the tag of the struct of the root.
.PP
With \f[CR]apart\f[R], the message holds no keys.
\f[CR]path\f[R] then checks the start of the message, the count of the
table and the tag of the root.
.PP
Each \f[CR]at_path\f[R] checks at each read:
.IP \(bu 2
the index of each step against the count of the list;
.IP \(bu 2
that each offset that it reads from the directory lies inside the
message;
.IP \(bu 2
the tag of the struct of each struct on the path;
.IP \(bu 2
the head of the field that it reads;
.IP \(bu 2
that a part of variable size lies inside the message and does not
overlap another part;
.IP \(bu 2
for a list of fixed\-size structs, the tag 32873 and its reference, and
that the length is a multiple of the size of the element.
.PP
\f[CR]view\f[R] checks the tag and the length of the typed array once.
After that, each element read checks only its index.
.PP
\f[CR]schema<T>::at_path\f[R] with no accessor makes the checks of
\f[CR]path\f[R] and of \f[CR]at_path\f[R] in one call.
.PP
The \f[CR]unverified_\f[R] forms make only the checks of a bound: that
each head, length, item and directory entry lies inside the message,
each index against its count, and the limits.
They skip the tags of the structs, the heads of the fields, the start of
the table, the fillers, the tag and the length check of a typed array,
the prefix of a list of fixed\-size structs, the check of a
\f[CR]bool\f[R] byte and, in \f[CR]unverified_decode\f[R], the order of
the items.
An accessor from \f[CR]unverified_path\f[R] gives only unverified
accessors.
\&../../reference/schema/unverified_path.md has the full list.
